module

public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Spaces.ClosedSubspaceFromQuasicoherentIdeal
public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Spaces.ZeroSchemeProperties

/-!
# The evaluation ideal of a section as a sheaf of ideals

Let `X` be an algebraic space over a scheme `S` contained in a big fppf site `B`, let `L` be an
`𝒪_X`-module and let `s` be a global section of `L`.  Stacks 0845 cuts out the zero scheme of
`s` by the quasi-coherent sheaf of ideals which is the image of `s : L^{⊗ -1} ⟶ 𝒪_X`; on an étale
chart `U` that ideal is `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.sectionEvaluationIdealAt`, the ideal generated
by the values `l(s|_U)` of the linear functionals on `L(U)`.  This file turns that chartwise
family into a *sheaf* of ideals of `𝒪_X` and isolates what is still missing before it is the
defining ideal sheaf of a closed subspace.

Two things are proved with no hypothesis.  First, the evaluation ideal is carried into the
evaluation ideal by restriction along a chart morphism over which the sections of `L` base
change; this is the only property of `L` the ideal's functoriality needs, and it is exactly
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.IsCartesianOnAffineCharts` read at one morphism.  Second, there is a
smallest sheaf of ideals of `𝒪_X` containing the evaluation ideal over every affine étale chart,
and it is the only candidate: any sheaf of ideals whose sections over the affine charts *are* the
evaluation ideals equals it.  So the residue of Stacks 0846 is a statement about one named
object.

**No Stacks 0846 tag is placed here** and no part of Stacks 0846 is claimed; see the
implementation notes for the two inputs that are missing and why each is needed.

## Main definitions

* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.sectionEvaluationSubmodule` is the family of ideals of `𝒪_X` generated
  by the evaluation ideals of `s` over the affine étale charts: over a chart `W` it is the ideal
  generated by the restrictions to `W` of the evaluation ideals of the affine charts `W` maps to,
  and it is stable under restriction.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.sectionEvaluationIdeal` is the sheaf of ideals of `𝒪_X` it generates,
  that is, the smallest sheaf submodule of `𝒪_X` above it.

## Main results

* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.sectionEvaluationIdealAt_map_le_of_bijective_sectionsBaseChange`:
  **where the sections of `L` base change, the evaluation ideal restricts into the evaluation
  ideal.**  A functional on `L(U)` is transported to one on `L(V)` through the base change
  comparison, and it takes the restricted section to the restricted value.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.sectionEvaluationSubmodule_obj_eq_of_isCartesianOnAffineCharts`:
  consequently, for a module cartesian on affine charts the family above has the evaluation
  ideal as its value over every affine étale chart.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.sectionEvaluationIdealAt_le_sectionEvaluationIdeal_obj` and
  `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.sectionEvaluationIdeal_le`: the sheaf of ideals contains the
  evaluation ideal over every affine étale chart, and it is the smallest sheaf of ideals that
  does.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.eq_sectionEvaluationIdeal_of_forall_isAffine`: **the candidate is
  unique.**  A sheaf of ideals of `𝒪_X` whose sections over every affine étale chart are the
  evaluation ideal of `s` is `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.sectionEvaluationIdeal`.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.exists_definingIdealAt_eq_sectionEvaluationIdealAt`: **Stacks 0846
  reduced to two statements about that one object** — that it is quasi-coherent, and that its
  sections over the affine étale charts are the evaluation ideals.  Granted those, a closed
  subspace of `X` has the evaluation ideal of `s` as its defining ideal on every affine chart,
  which is the hypothesis every assertion of
  `StacksFrontier/AlgebraicGeometry/Spaces/ZeroSchemeProperties.lean` carries.

## Implementation notes

The surjectivity half of Stacks 03MB is no longer the obstruction: by
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.exists_zeroSchemeSubfunctor_eq_of_isQuasicoherentOnSite` a
quasi-coherent sheaf of ideals of `𝒪_X` matching the evaluation ideals on the affine charts
already identifies the zero scheme.  What is missing is that *sheaf of ideals*, and it is
missing for two independent reasons, neither of which is about quasi-coherence of an ideal.

* **The chartwise evaluation ideals are not known to be stable under restriction.**  For a
  functional `l` on `L(U)` and a chart morphism `f : V ⟶ U` there is no reason for `l(s|_U)|_V`
  to be a value of a functional on `L(V)`: a functional on `L(U)` is transported to `L(V)` only
  through the base change comparison
  `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.sectionsBaseChange`, whose bijectivity for affine charts is
  `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.IsCartesianOnAffineCharts` of
  `StacksFrontier/AlgebraicGeometry/Spaces/ChartSections.lean:329`.  That is the forward
  implication of Descent, Proposition 03DX(2), whose essential-surjectivity residue is named
  `AlgebraicGeometry.SmallEtale.QuasicoherentIsEssImageAssociatedModule` at
  `StacksFrontier/AlgebraicGeometry/Descent/AssociatedModule/SmallEtaleEssentialImage.lean`.  This
  is why `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.sectionEvaluationSubmodule` has to be generated by the
  restrictions rather than being the chartwise family itself, and why its affine values are
  identified only under that hypothesis.
* **Even granted that, the sheaf of ideals it generates is not known to have the evaluation
  ideals as its affine values.**  Passing from the generated family to the sheaf it generates
  adds the sections which lie in it only locally, and discharging that addition by faithfully
  flat descent — as
  `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.surjective_sectionsBaseChange_submodule_unit_of_isQuasicoherentOnSite`
  does for a sheaf of ideals — needs the evaluation ideal over a smaller affine chart to be
  *generated* by the restrictions from the larger one, that is, the dual module of `L` to base
  change as well.  That holds where `L` is free of rank one, and the covering of an algebraic
  space by charts on which an invertible module is free of rank one is the descent clause of
  Stacks 0B8M, `Stacks.InvertibleLocallyFree.InvertibleIsLocal` at
  `StacksFrontier/AlgebraicGeometry/InvertibleLocallyFree/Basic.lean:349`, which is carried there as
  an undischarged hypothesis.

The same clause of Stacks 0B8M is what parts (3) and (4) of Stacks 0846 need beyond the
identification of the ideal: `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.IsLocallyPrincipal` and
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.IsEffectiveCartierDivisor` of Stacks 083B are conditions on `𝓘_{Z(s)}`
as a sheaf on the small étale site, and `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.isLocallyPrincipal_zeroScheme_iff`
and `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.isEffectiveCartierDivisor_zeroScheme_iff` transport them to the
prescribed-ideal subspace without asserting either; the step from the chartwise principality of
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.definingIdealAt_zeroScheme_isPrincipal` to a covering on whose members
the sheaf is generated by one section is the same missing local triviality, as recorded in the
section "What is not proved" of
`StacksFrontier/AlgebraicGeometry/Spaces/EffectiveCartierEtaleCharts.lean:60`.

The generated family is indexed by the *affine* charts only, which is what makes its affine
values identifiable: a morphism from an affine chart to a non-affine one is not covered by the
cartesian hypothesis, and the affine charts are in any case a dense subsite, so a sheaf of
ideals is determined by its sections over them
(`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.structureSubmodule_ext_of_forall_isAffine`).

The sheaf of ideals is built as the infimum, in the complete lattice of sheaf submodules of
`𝒪_X`, of those lying above the generated family, rather than by a sheafification construction;
the infimum of sheaf submodules is computed sectionwise, so the minimality and containment
statements below are immediate and no locality argument is needed to know that the result is a
sheaf.

No invertibility hypothesis on `L` appears, following
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.zeroSchemeSubfunctor_isClosedSubspace` and the results of
`StacksFrontier/AlgebraicGeometry/Spaces/ZeroSchemeProperties.lean`, which are also stated for a
section of an arbitrary `𝒪_X`-module.

